1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,571
Coach: A response can connect to someone's concern and still leave work to do.

2
00:00:05,571 --> 00:00:09,857
Coach: The next step is to notice exactly what remains unanswered,

3
00:00:09,857 --> 00:00:15,857
Coach: instead of treating the suggestion as either a complete solution or a total failure.

4
00:00:16,207 --> 00:00:20,493
Coach: The workshop plan still has thirty minutes and one facilitator.

5
00:00:20,493 --> 00:00:26,064
Coach: A visible step card has been suggested to help beginners find their place.

6
00:00:26,064 --> 00:00:29,064
Coach: It has not been tested or adopted.

7
00:00:29,064 --> 00:00:32,921
Coach: Listen to the concern Noah raises about the plan.

8
00:00:33,271 --> 00:00:38,414
Noah: A card might help, but if several pairs need help at once,

9
00:00:38,414 --> 00:00:40,985
Noah: one facilitator may not reach everyone.

10
00:00:41,335 --> 00:00:44,764
Coach: Noah has not said that cards are useless.

11
00:00:44,764 --> 00:00:50,764
Coach: He has identified a situation in which some people might still need direct help.

12
00:00:50,764 --> 00:00:56,764
Coach: The concern is how one facilitator would handle several requests at the same time.

13
00:00:57,114 --> 00:01:03,114
Coach: Saying the card will be very clear does not by itself answer that situation.

14
00:01:03,114 --> 00:01:06,114
Coach: Neither does repeating that practice is important.

15
00:01:06,114 --> 00:01:08,257
Coach: Those points may be relevant,

16
00:01:08,257 --> 00:01:12,114
Coach: but the remaining question is about access to help.

17
00:01:19,464 --> 00:01:24,178
Coach: A more targeted response offers a possible way to handle requests,

18
00:01:24,178 --> 00:01:27,607
Coach: or asks for information needed to compare ways.

19
00:01:27,607 --> 00:01:29,321
Coach: Here is one proposal.

20
00:01:29,321 --> 00:01:32,321
Coach: It is a starting point for discussion,

21
00:01:32,321 --> 00:01:36,178
Coach: not a claim that the issue has been solved.

22
00:01:36,528 --> 00:01:40,814
You: Could we pause after the first try and collect questions,

23
00:01:40,814 --> 00:01:45,957
You: so the facilitator can address a step that several pairs found difficult?

24
00:01:46,307 --> 00:01:49,307
Maya: That could help with a shared question.

25
00:01:49,307 --> 00:01:53,593
Maya: What if just one pair is stuck before the pause?

26
00:01:53,943 --> 00:01:59,514
Coach: The shared check might address a difficulty that several pairs have in common.

27
00:01:59,514 --> 00:02:04,229
Coach: Maya points out a different case: someone who needs help earlier.

28
00:02:04,229 --> 00:02:05,943
Coach: Both can be true.

29
00:02:05,943 --> 00:02:11,943
Coach: A useful response does not have to remove every possible difficulty in one sentence.

30
00:02:12,293 --> 00:02:16,150
Coach: You can now name the open question more precisely.

31
00:02:16,150 --> 00:02:18,722
Coach: What would participants do while waiting,

32
00:02:18,722 --> 00:02:22,579
Coach: and how would they signal that they need help?

33
00:02:22,579 --> 00:02:24,293
Coach: Those details need discussion.

34
00:02:24,293 --> 00:02:28,579
Coach: We do not yet know which arrangement would work best.

35
00:02:28,929 --> 00:02:31,929
Coach: Keep the task visible when you respond.

36
00:02:31,929 --> 00:02:35,358
Coach: Adding another facilitator would change the stated conditions;

37
00:02:35,358 --> 00:02:40,072
Coach: making the demonstration fill the session would remove time to try.

38
00:02:40,072 --> 00:02:44,786
Coach: A proposal must be checked against the resources and the purpose,

39
00:02:44,786 --> 00:02:46,929
Coach: not only against one concern.

40
00:02:47,279 --> 00:02:51,565
Coach: This does not mean a constraint can never be questioned.

41
00:02:51,565 --> 00:02:54,993
Coach: If the group is allowed to change it,

42
00:02:54,993 --> 00:02:59,279
Coach: say that explicitly and find out whether it is possible.

43
00:02:59,279 --> 00:03:04,422
Coach: Do not silently assume a resource that the task has not provided.

44
00:03:04,772 --> 00:03:10,343
Coach: Look again at the concern about someone needing help before the shared check.

45
00:03:10,343 --> 00:03:15,486
Coach: Privately form one question that would help the group examine that situation.

46
00:03:15,486 --> 00:03:18,486
Coach: It should seek information you still need,

47
00:03:18,486 --> 00:03:21,486
Coach: rather than repeat information already on screen.

48
00:03:33,836 --> 00:03:40,265
You: What could a pair do if they miss a step before we stop for questions?

49
00:03:40,615 --> 00:03:43,615
Coach: That question stays with the remaining difficulty

50
00:03:43,615 --> 00:03:47,472
Coach: and gives the group something specific to work on.

51
00:03:47,472 --> 00:03:50,472
Coach: A different question could be useful too.

52
00:03:50,472 --> 00:03:53,472
Coach: Check which uncertainty your question would reduce,

53
00:03:53,472 --> 00:03:57,329
Coach: and keep the answer open until someone provides it.

54
00:03:57,679 --> 00:04:01,965
Coach: Responding well includes noticing the limits of your own response.

55
00:04:01,965 --> 00:04:03,679
Coach: In the next section,

56
00:04:03,679 --> 00:04:09,679
Coach: we will practise updating a reply when someone says we understood the wrong issue.
